{
  "result": "Some mornings begin with an alarm, but the day itself starts later, in the quiet interval when the room is blue and the world has not yet remembered its demands. In that interval, ordinary objects become briefly mysterious: a glass of water holds the window, a sock on the floor resembles a small animal, and dust turns through sunlight like slow-moving stars.\n\nI like this unclaimed portion of the day because it asks for nothing. There is no performance yet, no polished answer to give. One can listen to the refrigerator hum, notice a neighbor\u2019s footsteps, or watch a sparrow argue with its reflection in a parked car. These details are not interruptions to life. They are life, unedited and wonderfully persistent.\n\nLater, the messages will arrive. Schedules will tighten, machines will request passwords, and someone will ask what happens next. We will answer as best we can, carrying the morning\u2019s quiet with us, even if only as a faint memory. Perhaps that is one purpose of paying attention: not to escape the busy world, but to meet it without becoming entirely hurried.\n\nA life is made of grand decisions, certainly, yet also of tiny permissions. We permit ourselves to pause, to look twice, to enjoy the first sip before the cup is empty. We permit wonder to remain practical. Then the morning moves on, not transformed exactly, but more inhabitable. And sometimes, that is enough: a brightness gathered from nothing dramatic, carried forward like a match sheltered from wind.",
